# Derived Ollama model for JTestForge - see usage.md, "Ollama (local model)".
#
# Build it once (no download: it reuses the base model's weights already on disk):
#   ollama create jtestforge-qwen3-coder -f tools/ollama/Modelfile
#
# Why a derived model at all: `ollama run` has no flag for num_ctx, temperature or
# num_predict, and Ollama silently drops the START of a prompt that does not fit num_ctx -
# which is where JTestForge puts the rules, the response contract and the class under test.
# Pinning the values in the model is the only way to set them when driving it by command.
#
# Change FROM to whichever model `ollama list` shows on your machine. This one is the official
# Q4_K_M build (18 GB, `ollama pull qwen3-coder:30b`). The smaller IQ3_XXS build
# (danielsheep/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct-1M-Unsloth:UD-IQ3_XXS, 12 GB) was tried first and
# is noticeably less reliable at following the response format.
FROM qwen3-coder:30b

# Must hold the prompt plus the answer: context.maxPromptChars (60000 chars is roughly
# 17-20k tokens of Java) + num_predict, with headroom. 32768 covers that; the KV cache
# for this model at 32k is on the order of a few GB on top of the weights.
PARAMETER num_ctx 32768

# The value the base model's authors recommend (with top_p 0.8, top_k 20, repeat_penalty
# 1.05, all inherited). 0.2 was tried first and the model fell into repetition loops on the
# web-slice units: the same 23 import lines over and over until num_predict ran out.
PARAMETER temperature 0.7

# Upper bound on the answer; a cut-off answer shows up as an unclosed ```java block,
# which ResponseParser rejects as a contract violation.
PARAMETER num_predict 4096
